.TH SU 1 
.SH NAME
su  \-  substitute user id temporarily
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B su
[ userid ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Su
demands the password of the specified
.I userid,
and if it is given,
changes to that 
.I userid
and invokes the Shell
.IR sh (1)
without changing the current directory or the
user environment (see
.IR environ (5)).
The new user ID stays in force until the Shell exits.
.PP
If no
.I userid
is specified, `root' is assumed.
To remind the super-user of his responsibilities,
the Shell substitutes `#' for its usual prompt.
.PP
.B Note:
that prompt is chosen by
.IR sh (1)
from the user id it finds itself running under, not from the environment.
.I Su
also rewrites a
.B PS1
already in the environment, which on this system there is not:
.IR login (1)
exports HOME, PATH and TERM and nothing else.
.PP
.B Note:
no password is demanded when the account has none, or when the caller
is already the super-user.
The test is on the
.I real
user id, which is what makes it right in a program that is set-user-id:
by the time it runs, the effective id is already 0.
.PP
.B Note:
.I su
is set-user-id to root, and it is one of the two programs of this system
for which that bit exists to change an identity rather than to reach a
directory operation.
.IR login (1)
is deliberately not set-user-id \- its privilege is inherited from
.IR getty (8)
\- so before
.I su
there was no way to change user from a shell at all.
.PP
.B Note:
the change is one-way.
.IR setuid (2)
here moves the real user id as well as the effective one, so the shell
.I su
starts cannot climb back; leaving it is the only way out, and
.IR sh (1)
leaves on end-of-file rather than on
.BR exit ,
which merely reprompts an interactive shell.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 1 for an unknown name, 2 for a wrong password
and 3 if the shell cannot be executed.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), login(1), newgrp(1), passwd(1), setuid(2), crypt(3)
